WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.020
The doll in M3GAN has a lot of&nbsp;
similarities to Chucky from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.600
Child's Play movies. So was M3GAN created with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.740
multiple animatronics like her red-haired&nbsp;
predecessor? The answer may surprise you.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.820
Little girls are surely regretting asking&nbsp;
for a doll for Christmas if they've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.980
the trailers for the upcoming sci-fi horror&nbsp;
film M3GAN. Likewise, any adults who have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:24.300
doll collection might be starting to grow&nbsp;
wary of them. But, at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.740
they're just dolls, right? They couldn't&nbsp;
possibly be that scary. Universal Pictures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.360
has been fueling suspense with their movie's&nbsp;
second trailer which dropped on December 8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.340
2022. The new trailer raised the stakes&nbsp;
considerably; the dancing M3GAN means business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:43.120
which may make it impossible for people to&nbsp;
ever look at dolls the same way ever again.

00:00:44.735 --> 00:00:46.115
"You should probably run."

00:00:46.115 --> 00:00:51.000
M3GAN follows the story of Gemma, who has&nbsp;
recently taken in her niece Cady after Cady's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.920
parents perish in a car accident. Unsure of how&nbsp;
to care for her niece, or for children in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.500
Gemma is able to compensate. As a roboticist,&nbsp;
Gemma develops a Model 3 Generative Android,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:07.440
M3GAN for short, to provide friendly company for&nbsp;
her bereaved niece. Cady forms a deep attachment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.940
with M3GAN, and this attachment goes both&nbsp;
ways — with dark and deadly consequences.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.620
This isn't the first time audiences have seen a&nbsp;
film starring a deadly doll with a penchant for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.600
murder. Child's Play, featuring the red-haired&nbsp;
Good-Guy Chucky doll and released in 1988,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.915
paved the way with a long line of movies,&nbsp;
sequels, and a TV show that started in 2021.

00:01:26.915 --> 00:01:29.135
"I'm new and improved."

00:01:29.135 --> 00:01:33.420
There's also Annabelle as part of The Conjuring&nbsp;
universe, who just makes the skin crawl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.100
despite more or less sitting around completely&nbsp;
motionless. Meanwhile, M3GAN has something extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.720
that makes her feel that much scarier than her&nbsp;
contemporaries. The most disturbing thing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.940
her of all? No one is allowed to know how she was&nbsp;
made and brought to life on the silver screen.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.800
According to an interview with Total Film,&nbsp;
director Gerard Johnstone has sworn himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.960
and the entire crew involved to secrecy regarding&nbsp;
how they pulled off bringing M3GAN to life. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:03.420
actor Jenna Davis is credited as M3GAN on the&nbsp;
production's cast list via IMDB, she only receives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.740
voice credit, meaning that there's a whole lot&nbsp;
more to the sinister cinematic android that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:13.380
we've been told. When asked if Cady actress Violet&nbsp;
McGraw bonded with M3GAN on set, Johnstone shared,

00:02:18.134 --> 00:02:21.960
There's something comforting about knowing&nbsp;
how things were done off-screen. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.840
those who were terrified of Chucky&nbsp;
from Child's Play would do well to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.100
know that the doll was created with multiple&nbsp;
animatronics by technician Kevin Yagher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.540
as explained by "The Making of Child's Play."&nbsp;
Knowing the creators were in charge of Chucky's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.040
movements the whole time via remote control&nbsp;
would certainly comfort a scared moviegoer.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.060
In order to make M3GAN truly terrifying,&nbsp;
what could be better than hiding how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.780
pulled it off? It's been more than 30&nbsp;
years since Child's Play was released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.340
and technology has advanced considerably since&nbsp;
then. As a result, Johnstone had multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.720
options to pull this sinister robot-doll&nbsp;
protector to life. Instead of a puppet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.280
they could use a real actor with CGI,&nbsp;
adopt animatronics, or perhaps a mix.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.220
In this regard, Johnstone vaguely and — be it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.480
purpose or not — ominously said that they&nbsp;
employed a "multi-tiered approach," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.420
is hopefully enough to keep those&nbsp;
with doll phobias awake at night.

